## Migration: `Thread.Abort` Timeout → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code forcibly aborts a thread after a timeout. In .NET 8, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006). The replacement is `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter` with task-based execution.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Preferred: async overload
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout, CancellationToken externalToken = default)
    {
        using var cts = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(externalToken);
        cts.CancelAfter(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await Task.Run(work, cts.Token).WaitAsync(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (externalToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw; // Caller cancelled — propagate as-is
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Guidance |
|---------|----------|
| **Work doesn't actually stop** | Cooperative cancellation only works if `work` checks the token. If `work` is a tight CPU loop or blocking I/O, it will continue running in the background after the timeout fires. |
| **Leaking `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always wrap in `using`. Leaking causes timer/callback leaks. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch it at the orchestration boundary to convert to `TimeoutException`. |
| **Uncooperative code** | If `work` calls third-party code that can't accept a token, the task becomes a "fire and forget" — you get your timeout, but the background work keeps running. For truly uncooperative code, move it to a child `Process` and call `Process.Kill`. |

### Making Work Cancellable

For the timeout to actually stop work, pass the token through:

```csharp
// Before: opaque delegate
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(() => ComputeExpensiveResult(), TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

// After: cancellation-aware delegate
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    return Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token)
               .WaitAsync(cts.Token)
               .GetAwaiter().GetResult();
}

// Caller cooperates:
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in largeCollection)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        Process(item);
    }
    return result;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
```

### What Was Removed

| .NET Framework API | Replacement |
|--------------------|-------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` / `CancelAfter` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |
| Manual `new Thread` + `Join(timeout)` | `Task.Run` + `WaitAsync(token)` |

The fundamental shift: cancellation is now **cooperative** — the work must opt in by checking the token. There is no way to forcibly terminate managed code in modern .NET without killing the process.
